# SDDKit Workflow Rules

These rules define the mandatory development workflow for this repository.

## The Five Stages (in order)

```
/spec → /plan → /tasks → /implement → /review
```

You must complete and get human approval for each stage before proceeding to
the next. Do not skip stages.

## Stage Enforcement

### Before /plan
- `specs/<feature>/spec.md` must exist and be approved.
- Run: `sddkit approve <feature> spec`

### Before /tasks
- `specs/<feature>/plan.md` must exist and be approved.
- Run: `sddkit approve <feature> plan`

### Before /implement
- ALL THREE stages must be approved:
  ```bash
  cat specs/<feature>/status.json
  # All three: "approved": true
  ```
- `sddkit check <feature>` must exit 0.
- **If either check fails, do NOT write any source code.**

### Before /review
- All implementation tasks must be marked `[x]` in `tasks.md`.
- All tests must pass: `pytest`
- `sddkit check <feature>` must exit 0.

## Requirement Traceability (Cursor Enforcement)

> **Note:** Unlike Claude Code, Cursor does not have real-time hook-based
> gate enforcement. Enforcement is handled by:
> 1. `sddkit check <feature>` (run manually or in pre-commit)
> 2. CI (`sddkit eval` in GitHub Actions)
> 3. These always-apply rules

Always cite REQ-NNN IDs in plan steps, task descriptions, and test names.
The traceability checker will fail CI if any REQ-ID is uncovered.

## Writing Specs

- Every requirement must have a unique ID: `REQ-NNN` (3 digits, zero-padded).
- Example: `REQ-001`, `REQ-042`, `REQ-100`
- Duplicate IDs are a hard error in `sddkit check`.

## Commit Messages

Use this format so requirements are traceable in git history:
```
feat(<feature>): implement REQ-001 — <short description>
test(<feature>): add tests for REQ-001 and REQ-002
fix(<feature>): correct edge case in REQ-003 handler
```

## Reporting

After implementation, run:
```bash
sddkit report <feature>
```
This shows the audit log summary (what was allowed, what was blocked).
